Subject: [#94-12-484] Extinction

 goetz@cs.Buffalo.EDU (Phil Goetz) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-12-428:
>A couple of people have picked up on this.  As I said, the measurement of
>complexity is complex.  If you study complexity, you know that
>complexity seems to be maximal in systems where sudden changes have a 
>power-law distribution, meaning in part that small changes are frequent,
>and big (destructive) changes are infrequent.  Occasional destruction is
>necessary to create complexity; the trick is in walking the tightrope
>between order and chaos.  There's a whole field of study on this; I
>refer interested people to anything published by the Santa Fe Institute
>(marketed through Addison Wesley), esp. Artificial Life II.  Also some
>stuff in back issues of Physica D.

 This reminds me of something that Tom Ray discovered while playing with
his artificial life program, Tierra. The number of species shows the same
gradual rise punctuated by infrequent large crashes that the fossil record
shows, even though there are no external influences in Tierra that could
trigger extinctions. It appears that the main cause of extinctions is
that important innovations enable the species possesing that innovation
to outcompete a lot of existing species.
 I claim that human intelligence is one of those major innovations, and
that we shouldn't be surprised that there has been a dramatic jump in the
extinction rate.
 I also claim that using the number of species as a measure of diversity
or complexity gives misleading results when comparing times of rapid
evolutionary change to relatively stable times. The new species tend to
be more different from previous species than were the species that go
extinct.
 These are some of the reasons why I yawn when people complain that the
free market is causing mass extinctions.
