As I said, the trick is to walk the tightrope between chaos and
order.  If our society were structured so that mass extinctions like
the one we've had these past 10,000 years happened once every
sixty million years or so, that would indicate we were on the right
track.  If our society is structured so that mass extinction is a
condition of its existence, that shows we are too far into the disordered,
entropic regime.

There's a book, _The End of Evolution_, which Peter perhaps ought to read
(though I doubt he will) which argues that the rate of extinction at
present is greater than at any mass extinction in the past.  This is
because the author thinks that mass extinctions took thousands of years;
he presents his reasons in the book.  He doesn't argue against an
asteroid strike at the K-T boundary, but as Peter has read some ALife
literature he should realize that an asteroid strike might not have
caused the extinctions directly, but pushed the world into the disordered
region after which die-offs continued for thousands of years.

